# HexSpindle @VERSION@ — source package

HexSpindle is a data transformation and forensics workbench that runs entirely in your browser. This package holds
the source needed to run HexSpindle on your own computer, or to build the same programs that are published on the
[releases page](https://github.com/HexSpindle/HexSpindle/releases). If you just want to use HexSpindle, the
ready-made download for your system (`.exe`, `.app`, `.deb`, `.rpm` or `.tar.gz`) is simpler: it needs nothing
installed.

The repository's test suite and CI configuration are not included; they are in the
[HexSpindle repository](https://github.com/HexSpindle/HexSpindle).

## What is in this package

| Path | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `index.html`, `app.js`, `app.css`, `core/`, `modules/`, `assets/` | The HexSpindle web app. It runs as-is; no build step is needed. |
| `analytics/` | The app's anonymous usage-analytics client and its privacy notice (see [Privacy](#privacy)). |
| `licenses/`, `LICENSE`, `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`, `DATA_SOURCE_NOTICES.md` | License texts and notices. |
| `tools/build-site.mjs`, `tools/vendor-inventory.json`, `tools/package.json`, `tools/package-lock.json` | Builds the optimised site (one bundle instead of hundreds of modules) and its third-party notice file. Needs Node.js. |
| `desktop/launcher/` | The HexSpindle launcher: a small local web server, written in Go, that serves the app and opens your browser. |
| `desktop/build.sh`, `desktop/tools/`, `desktop/packaging/` | Build scripts, icons and packaging metadata for the release downloads. |
| `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, `.nojekyll`, `google8833fe62b3f6acca.html` | Files of the public website; kept because the site build copies them. |

## Start HexSpindle from source

You need **[Go](https://go.dev/dl/) 1.27.2 or newer**. (An older Go from 1.21 on downloads the right version by
itself the first time.) Nothing else is required: the launcher serves the app straight from this folder.

The launcher prints the address, opens it in your default browser, and stops on its own about 15 seconds after the
last HexSpindle tab is closed. To stop it sooner, press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> in the terminal.

### Windows

Open **PowerShell** (or Command Prompt) in the folder where you extracted this package, then:

```powershell
cd desktop
go run ./launcher --site ..
```

The launcher listens only on `127.0.0.1`, which other computers cannot reach, so it needs no firewall exception.

### macOS

Open **Terminal** in the folder where you extracted this package, then:

```bash
cd desktop
go run ./launcher --site ..
```

Go is available from [go.dev/dl](https://go.dev/dl/) or Homebrew (`brew install go`).

### Linux

Open a terminal in the folder where you extracted this package, then:

```bash
cd desktop
go run ./launcher --site ..
```

Install Go from [go.dev/dl](https://go.dev/dl/) if your distribution's `golang` package is older than 1.21. The
browser is opened with `xdg-open`; on a machine without a desktop, add `--no-browser` and open the printed
address yourself.

### Then

Your browser opens **http://127.0.0.1:47800/**. With `--site`, files are read from disk on every request, so if
you edit the source, reload the page to see the change. Add `--keep-running` while you work on the code, so the
launcher does not stop when you close the tab.

## Use the optimised build

Served from source, the app loads several hundred module files. The release downloads serve an optimised build
instead (a single bundle plus the libraries that load on demand), which starts faster. To serve that build, you also
need **[Node.js](https://nodejs.org/) 24** (the version HexSpindle is tested with).

Windows (PowerShell):

```powershell
cd tools
npm ci
node build-site.mjs --out ..\dist
cd ..\desktop
go run ./launcher --site ..\dist
```

macOS and Linux:

```bash
cd tools
npm ci
node build-site.mjs --out ../dist
cd ../desktop
go run ./launcher --site ../dist
```

`npm ci` installs the exact tool versions in `tools/package-lock.json` into `tools/node_modules`. The site is
built into `dist/`. The source files themselves are never modified.

## Build your own single-file program

To get a program like the release downloads, with the site built in so it runs without this folder, first build the
site (see above), then copy it into the launcher and compile. The commands below produce a program for the system
you are on. To build for another one, set `GOOS` and `GOARCH` first (for example `GOOS=windows GOARCH=arm64` in
bash, or `$env:GOOS = "linux"; $env:GOARCH = "arm64"` in PowerShell).

Windows (PowerShell):

```powershell
cd desktop
go run ./tools/prepare-site --dist ..\dist --out launcher\site
go build -trimpath -ldflags "-s -w -H=windowsgui -X main.version=@VERSION@" -o HexSpindle.exe ./launcher
.\HexSpindle.exe
```

`-H=windowsgui` makes a program that opens without a console window when double-clicked, like the release
download. Leave it out if you want to see the launcher's output when you start it from Explorer.

macOS and Linux:

```bash
cd desktop
go run ./tools/prepare-site --dist ../dist --out launcher/site
go build -trimpath -ldflags "-s -w -X main.version=@VERSION@" -o hexspindle ./launcher
./hexspindle
```

`prepare-site` compresses the site and adds the small script the launcher uses to notice when the last tab
closes. The program does not use `launcher/site` after it is built; you can move it
anywhere. (On macOS, a program you build yourself is signed automatically by the Go linker on Apple Silicon and is
not quarantined, so it runs without the Gatekeeper steps the downloaded app needs.)

### Build every release package

`desktop/build.sh` builds the complete set of downloads, exactly as the release pipeline does: Windows `.exe`
(x64 and ARM64, with icon and version information), the macOS universal `.app`, Linux `.tar.gz`, `.deb` and
`.rpm` (x64 and ARM64), this source package, and `SHA256SUMS.txt`. It runs on Linux and macOS, and on Windows in
Git Bash, and needs Go and a built site:

```bash
bash desktop/build.sh --dist dist --out packages --version @VERSION@
```

The source package step reads the file list from git, so run it from a git checkout of the repository. The macOS
app is given its ad-hoc signature only when `RCODESIGN` points to an
[rcodesign](https://github.com/indygreg/apple-platform-rs/releases) binary; without it the `.app` will not open on
Apple Silicon Macs.

## Launcher options

```
--site <folder>             serve this folder (the source folder, or a built site) instead of the built-in copy
--port <n>                  use this port (default 47800, or the next free one up to 47809)
--no-browser                do not open a browser tab; just print the address
--keep-running              keep running after the last HexSpindle tab is closed (stop with Ctrl+C)
--idle-timeout <duration>   for tabs that cannot hold a connection: treat one as closed after this long (default 30m)
--exit-grace <duration>     wait this long after the last tab closes before exiting (default 15s)
--first-connect-timeout <duration>
                            exit if no tab opens the app within this time (default 5m)
--feeds-upstream <url>      site that feed data is fetched from (default https://hexspindle.github.io)
--no-analytics              turn off the app's anonymous usage analytics
--version                   print the version and exit
--licenses                  print the launcher's license notices and exit
```

Pass them after `go run ./launcher`, for example `go run ./launcher --site .. --port 48000 --keep-running`.

## Good to know

- **Saved data.** Recipes and settings live in your browser's storage for the address
  `http://127.0.0.1:47800`. Every run uses the same address, so they are still there next time. A different
  `--port` is a different address, with its own, separate storage.
- **Already running.** If HexSpindle is already running (for example the downloaded app), starting it again just
  opens a new tab on the running copy, even with `--site`. Close the HexSpindle tabs (or press Ctrl+C in its
  terminal) first, or give the new run another `--port`.
- **Feed data.** Operations that use threat-intelligence feeds read them from `data/feeds/`, which is not part of
  the source (the public website refreshes them daily). The launcher fetches them from
  https://hexspindle.github.io when an operation asks, so those operations need an internet connection. All other
  processing happens locally in your browser.
- **Port in use.** If another program uses ports 47800–47809, choose a free port with `--port`.
- **Log.** Each run writes a short log to `HexSpindle/launcher.log` in your user cache folder
  (`%LocalAppData%` on Windows, `~/Library/Caches` on macOS, `~/.cache` on Linux).

## Other web servers

The app is a static site, so other web servers can serve this folder too, for example
`python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1`. Keep in mind:

- The server must send `.js` and `.mjs` files as `text/javascript` and `.wasm` files as `application/wasm`.
  Some servers on Windows take these types from the registry, where `.js` is often mapped to `text/plain`; the
  browser then refuses to run the app and the page stays blank (the browser console reports a MIME type error).
- Feed-based operations will not find `data/feeds/`, and the launcher's automatic stop does not apply.
- Usage analytics stay off: the app sends them only from the public website and from pages the launcher serves.

## Privacy

All processing of your data happens in your browser, on your computer. Like the public website, HexSpindle served
by the launcher sends **anonymous usage analytics**: which operations are used, whether they succeed and how long
they take, never your inputs, outputs, files or recipe arguments. `analytics/privacy.html` (linked from the app's
footer) describes exactly what is collected. Analytics are on by default, and there are three ways to turn them
off:

- click **Opt out** in the app's footer (remembered for the address `http://127.0.0.1:47800`),
- turn on Global Privacy Control or Do Not Track in your browser, or
- start the launcher with `--no-analytics`, for example `go run ./launcher --site .. --no-analytics`.

The analytics are sent to the endpoint in the `hexspindle-analytics-endpoint` line of `index.html`.

## Verify a download

Each release lists the SHA-256 of every file in `SHA256SUMS.txt`, and each file has a build-provenance attestation
that ties it to the GitHub Actions run that built it from the tagged commit. To check this package:

```bash
sha256sum --check --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS.txt
gh attestation verify HexSpindle-@VERSION@-source.tar.gz --repo HexSpindle/HexSpindle
```

On Windows, `Get-FileHash HexSpindle-@VERSION@-source.zip` prints the SHA-256 to compare with the one in
`SHA256SUMS.txt`; on macOS, `shasum -a 256 HexSpindle-@VERSION@-source.tar.gz` does the same. The `gh` command
needs the [GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com/).

## License

HexSpindle is released under the MIT License (see `LICENSE`). Bundled third-party code and data keep their own
licenses, listed in `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`, `DATA_SOURCE_NOTICES.md` and `licenses/`. Programs built with Go also
contain the Go runtime and standard library, distributed under the BSD-style license in
`desktop/launcher/legal/GO-LICENSE.txt` (printed by `--licenses`).
